<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#germanisation">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Germanisation</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Germanization</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Germanisation is the spread of the German language, people and culture into other lands, through settlement or state policy. In modern history the term refers above all to Prussian and German policies in Polish lands from the 1830s to 1918, including language laws and the Settlement Commission founded in 1886, and to Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s brutal plans for occupied eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/germanisation.jpg" alt="Germanisation" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hstoops · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:German_Conservatism_Flags_Symbol.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Germanisation covers different forms and degrees of the expansion of the German language and of elements of German culture. There are cases of complete assimilation, as with the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> in the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> in the 11th century, and of voluntary adoption, as in the law of Imperial and present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, which follows the German model. It took place through cultural contact, by the political decision of the adopting party, or by force. In linguistics the word also describes non-German languages taking in many German words. In Slavic countries it is often understood as the acculturation of Slavic and Baltic speakers after conquest by or contact with Germans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Early Germanisation went with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostsiedlung">Ostsiedlung</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, in lands formerly inhabited by Slavic tribes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obotrites">Obotrites</a>, Veleti and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorbs">Sorbs</a>. Historians note that this settlement did not include deliberate Germanisation. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> decimated and resettled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Old Prussians</a>, and their language became extinct in the 17th century. After the 1620 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">Battle of White Mountain</a> the Bohemian lands saw two centuries of recatholicisation and growing influence of German-speaking elites. Emperor Joseph II decreed that German would replace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as the official language of government.</p><p>In the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and, to a lesser degree, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> imposed German culture and language on non-German peoples, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> in particular. After 1815 Prussia's king promised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Posen</a> the use of their language, and policy eased in 1815–30. About 350,000 Poles moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> in the late 19th century, where they were watched by the authorities and many changed their names to Germanised forms. Under the Nazis the policy had an ethnic and racial meaning, and plans assumed that up to 50 per cent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> could be Germanised while 80 to 85 per cent of Poles were to be deported to western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> an estimated 50,000 to 200,000 children judged racially acceptable were taken from their families to be raised as Germans, and at least 10,000 of them are estimated to have been murdered after being judged unfit. Only 10 to 15 per cent returned to their families after the war. The pressure of Germanisation provoked national movements among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, Slovene speakers and others in the 19th century. Josef Dobrovský's first modern grammar of Czech, from 1809, was published in German because Czech was not used in academic writing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Germanisation” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their pagan culture was “still in full working order” in the 11th century, when an uprising broke out in the 1030s, but in the 12th century Catholicism was imposed by force in the Northern Crusades, bound up with <b>Germanisation</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Maria Konopnicka, a poet and activist for Polish independence, wrote &quot;Rota&quot; (&quot;The Oath&quot;) in 1908 while staying in Cieszyn, as a protest against the German Empire's policy of forced <b>Germanisation</b> of the Poles in the lands Prussia had annexed in the partitions of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rota">Rota</a></li><li><blockquote>After the November Uprising of 1830 in Congress Poland, the provincial president Eduard Flottwell began a policy of <b>Germanisation</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-duchy-of-posen">Grand Duchy of Posen</a></li></ul></section>
